From tercer at ucla.edu  Fri Mar  4 08:07:09 2005
From: tercer at ucla.edu (James Honaker)
Date: Fri, 4 Mar 2005 05:07:09 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [amelia] integrating amelia imputation without stata
In-Reply-To: <001401c3b6dd$1f65d1a0$68058ec6@pennytd>
References: <001401c3b6dd$1f65d1a0$68058ec6@pennytd>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.60.0503040459310.14456@weber.sscnet.ucla.edu>


While the MI package will automatically combine estimates and standard 
errors from analysis models across datasets, the formulas are not 
themselves very complicated (I hope Ken Scheve doesn't take that as any 
knock against the usefulness of those procedures).

Run your analysis, or compute your means, or whatever parameters you are 
interested in, in each dataset, then average these to get your quantity of 
interest.  For the standard errors in these estimates, the formula has 
a second term.  You need to average the standard errors, and also add 
uncertainty if the different datasets are predicting different model 
parameters.  The formula is in:

http://gking.harvard.edu/files/evil.pdf

in equation (3).  It is the same formula used in model averaging, only 
here the model weights are equal, since each imputed dataset is equally 
valid.

regards,
James Honaker

